{% extends "base.html.jinja" %}
{% block content %}
<h1>About the data and its limits</h1>
<p class="lede">
  {{ site_name }} republishes state harmful algal bloom advisory records,
  one page per water, with each water's full history: California's since
  2016{% if "MO" in states_present %} and Missouri's since 2017{% endif %}. It adds nothing the states did not record.
  Where EPA has a satellite estimate for a large lake, it is shown separately
  and labeled as one.
</p>

<h2 id="limits">What the data cannot tell you</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Absence of an advisory does not mean a water is safe.</strong> Most lakes and rivers are never tested, reports come from the public and partner agencies on a voluntary basis, and the state says its data "is not exhaustive of all ambient conditions across the state."</li>
  <li><strong>An advisory is a recommendation.</strong> In the state's words, "a recommended advisory may or may not result in a posting of the advisory on the ground." Here, "posted" means published in the state's records.</li>
  <li><strong>End dates are often blank.</strong> "Advisory 'End Date' can be blank for a long time." A blank end date is unknown, never "ended".</li>
  <li><strong>Timing.</strong> The pipeline reads the states' data once a day and the satellite estimates once a week. The state's own map is updated daily. Check the <a href="{{ source.official_map_url }}">official map</a> and posted signs before you go.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="after-contact">After contact with the water</h2>
<p>{{ human_advice }} For a dog, see <a href="{{ root }}dogs/">dogs and toxic algae</a>.</p>
<p>{{ not_medical_advice }}</p>

<p>Other pages cover things this one does not. <a href="{{ root }}where-to-check/">Where to check a California algae advisory</a> lists them, with the date we read each.</p>

<h2 id="status">How a status is decided</h2>
<p>Each report is one spot at a water. For each spot, the latest record that decides anything wins:</p>
<ul>
  <li>An advisory (Caution, Warning, Danger, or another sign type the state uses) means the spot reads "last posted &hellip;, no all-clear recorded" until the state records an end.</li>
  <li>The state's "None" record (no advisory recommended) or an end date the state recorded ends it, with that date.</li>
  <li>"NA - refer to Report Details" and blank levels change nothing.</li>
</ul>
<p>A water shows an advisory if any of its spots does, at the highest level among them. Rows the pipeline could not place honestly are left out and counted: {{ quarantine.total }} in the current file{% for reason, n in quarantine.by_reason.items() %}{% if loop.first %} ({% else %}; {% endif %}{{ n }} {{ reason }}{% if loop.last %}){% endif %}{% endfor %}.</p>

<h2 id="sources">Sources and licenses</h2>
<p>Each source records the end of an advisory in its own way, and only an explicit end counts. Official advisories and satellite estimates are separate kinds of record and are never mixed.</p>
{% for s in all_sources %}
<h3 id="{{ s.id }}">{{ s.agency }}</h3>
<dl>
  <dt>What it is</dt><dd>{{ s.program }} ({{ "satellite estimate" if s.record_kind == "satellite_estimate" else "official advisory records" }})</dd>
  <dt>Data</dt><dd><a href="{{ s.dataset_url }}">{{ s.dataset_url }}</a></dd>
  <dt>License</dt><dd>{{ s.license }}; {{ s.rights }} (read {{ s.license_read_on|date }})</dd>
  <dt>How an end is recorded</dt><dd>{{ s.lift_explanation }}</dd>
  {% for f in source_files if f.source == s.id %}
  <dt>Last read</dt><dd>{{ f.file_name }} ({{ f.rows_read }} rows), {{ f.fetched_at[:10]|date }}</dd>
  {% endfor %}
</dl>
<p>{{ s.attribution }}</p>
{% if s.required_disclaimer %}
<div class="disclaimer">
  <p><strong>Disclaimer required by the State of {{ state_names.get(s.state, s.state) }}:</strong></p>
  <p>{{ s.required_disclaimer }}</p>
</div>
{% endif %}
{% if s.publisher_caveat %}
<p>The {{ s.agency }} says of its map: &ldquo;{{ s.publisher_caveat }}&rdquo; <a href="{{ s.publisher_caveat_url }}">Source</a>.</p>
{% endif %}
{% if s.revocable %}
<p class="muted">The publisher's terms let it withdraw permission: &ldquo;{{ s.revocation_clause }}&rdquo; If it does, this site removes that state's data.</p>
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}

{% if ny_history %}
<h3 id="ny-dec-habs-yearly">New York State Department of Environmental Conservation</h3>
<p>New York pages are a different kind of record: DEC's yearly summary of the waters where it confirmed a harmful algal bloom, with the first and last report dates each year. They are history only. They show no current status, a last report date is not an end date, and {{ site_name }} sends no alerts for them. <a href="{{ root }}state/ny/">New York bloom history</a> explains what the summary does and does not say.</p>
<p>{{ ny_attribution }}</p>
{% endif %}

<h2 id="satellite">About the satellite estimates</h2>
<p>{{ satellite_explainer }}</p>

<h2 id="app">The iPhone app</h2>
<p>A paid iPhone app is in development. Browsing and every lake's history will be free; alerts for your favorite lakes and a Home Screen widget will be a one-time purchase. The app checks this site's data file about once a day, so an alert comes within about a day of the state publishing a new advisory, not in real time.</p>
{% endblock %}
